68:\section{Introduction}
69:\IEEEPARstart{T}{he} rapid advent of Connected and Autonomous Vehicles (CAVs) and Cooperative Intelligent Transportation Systems (C-ITS) is fundamentally transforming modern vehicular networks~\cite{al-khasawneh2026uav}. For safety-critical applications such as cooperative collision avoidance, platooning, and intelligent intersection management, it is imperative for the infrastructure to maintain highly accurate and real-time mobility data—such as the position, velocity, and acceleration—of surrounding vehicles~\cite{zhang2025small, li2025next}. To guarantee the reliability of these advanced ITS services, vehicles must continuously exchange their dynamic state information with the infrastructure, making real-time data tracking a cornerstone of future vehicular networks.
71:However, accommodating this massive influx of real-time mobility data poses severe challenges to existing communication architectures. While relying purely on Cellular Vehicle-to-Network (V2N) communications provides wide coverage, it incurs prohibitive monetary costs and suffers from backhaul latency bottlenecks~\cite{sun2025overcoming}. Consequently, managing local mobility information through Roadside Units (RSUs) via Vehicle-to-Infrastructure (V2I) communication is considered a practical and scalable alternative. Yet, even within the localized coverage of an RSU, if vehicles blindly broadcast their status at high, fixed frequencies (e.g., 10 Hz Cooperative Awareness Messages in ETSI standards), it inevitably leads to catastrophic wireless channel congestion and packet collisions~\cite{park2025adaptive}. Furthermore, such purely periodic transmissions recklessly drain the limited energy resources of vehicles, presenting a critical bottleneck for the sustainability of real-time V2I tracking~\cite{duan2026energy}.
74:Existing AoI-aware scheduling approaches~\cite{mlika2022deep, qi2025deep, zhang2025drl} typically treat the update decision as a simple binary choice or optimize only a single dimension (e.g., update interval or power) in isolation.
96:\section{Related Work}
99:\subsection{Age of Information in Vehicular Networks}
100:The concept of AoI was originally introduced for status update systems~\cite{kaul2012real} and has since been extensively studied in vehicular networks.
102:Mlika and Cherkaoui~\cite{mlika2022deep} applied DDPG to minimize AoI in cellular V2X communications, demonstrating the feasibility of DRL-based scheduling for vehicular scenarios.
103:Qi \emph{et~al.}~\cite{qi2025deep} proposed a SAC-based resource allocation scheme for RIS-aided IoV networks, incorporating AoI awareness into the reward design.
104:Arani \emph{et~al.}~\cite{arani2026resilient} developed a resilient AoI-aware optimization framework using DRL for intelligent transportation systems.
105:Zhang \emph{et~al.}~\cite{zhang2025drl} jointly optimized AoI and energy consumption in C-V2X enabled IoV using DRL.
107:\subsection{Resource Allocation in V2X Networks}
108:Azizi \emph{et~al.}~\cite{azizi2024efficient} proposed a multi-agent RL mechanism for AoI-aware resource management in VLC-V2X networks.
109:Lin \emph{et~al.}~\cite{lin2025optimization} optimized spectrum resource allocation for vehicle platoons using DRL.
